          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Werner Forssmann's Early Career</strong><br>\nWerner Forssmann, born in 1904 in Germany, trained as a urologist but became interested in cardiology. His curiosity led him to experiment with a procedure that would later become a cornerstone of modern medicine: cardiac catheterization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pioneering Cardiac Catheterization</strong><br>\nIn a daring self-experiment in 1929, Forssmann inserted a catheter into his own heart via a vein in his arm. This risky procedure proved that such an approach could be safe and laid the groundwork for future diagnostic and surgical techniques.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Overcoming Medical Skepticism</strong><br>\nForssmann's work was initially met with skepticism and he struggled to find support among his peers. However, his persistence and eventual success demonstrated the potential of his methods, changing the face of cardiac care.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
